1
00:00:00,920 --> 00:00:02,380
Hi, I'm Jason Siever.

2
00:00:03,340 --> 00:00:04,080
I'm a psychiatrist.

3
00:00:04,460 --> 00:00:07,220
I've spent the last 15 years helping people with their problems.

4
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:08,600
And I'm Maggie Siever.

5
00:00:08,940 --> 00:00:12,700
I've spent the last 15 years helping our kids with problems even Jason wouldn't

6
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:16,860
believe. Now Maggie has gone back to work as a reporter for the local newspaper.

7
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:20,480
And Jason has moved his practice into the house so he can be there for the kids.

8
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:21,440
They're great kids.

9
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:22,600
Most of the time.

10
00:00:22,780 --> 00:00:24,000
And the rest of the time?

11
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:26,220
We love them anyway.

12
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:29,000
Yeah. Unbelievable. What?

13
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:42,300
What? What? What?

14
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:46,680
What? What? to begin.

15
00:00:47,320 --> 00:00:50,060
As long as we got each other,

16
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:59,120
we got the world spinning right at our hands, baby, rain or shine all the time.

17
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:03,730
We got each other sharing the map to love.

18
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:13,280
Dun, dun, dun, dun, dun, dun, dun, dun, dun, dun, dun, dun, dun.

19
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:16,100
Dun dun dun dun dun dun dun.

20
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:18,520
Dun dun dun dun dun dun.

21
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:22,720
Dun dun dun dun dun dun dun dun.

22
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:25,760
All right, lady, drop that spatula or you're scrimmed.

23
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:28,920
Go ahead. Make my day.

24
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:39,920
Mmm. Well, I guess I showed you.

25
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:42,880
Oh. Show me more.

26
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:45,400
Jason, with kids.

27
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:48,840
I can kiss the kids later.

28
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:52,240
You know, I read an article.

29
00:01:52,520 --> 00:01:58,000
It said that two career couples should really make special effort to always remain

30
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,920
frisky. At breakfast?

31
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:05,100
At all meals.

32
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:10,400
What's the matter?

33
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:11,460
You guys aren't getting enough?

34
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:16,500
Mike, a lot of kids would get smacked for a remark like that.

35
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:18,240
Come on, Dad, you can't hit me.

36
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:19,600
You're a liberal humanist.

37
00:02:20,780 --> 00:02:21,660
Could be an accident.

38
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:25,060
Could be a dream come true.

39
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:28,400
Mom, can't we sell Carol and get a tape deck for the bull, though?

40
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:31,280
Mike, you give new meaning to the word vacuous.

41
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:35,060
Oh, yeah? What was the old meaning?

42
00:02:36,860 --> 00:02:38,440
I'll rest. My case.

43
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:47,080
Ben! Ben! What's so funny, Ben?

44
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:51,160
That Phyllis George, she's screwed up again.

45
00:02:54,060 --> 00:02:55,360
Hey, what's that you're reading about?

46
00:02:56,340 --> 00:03:01,060
Well, it says here that as the universe expands, all matter is slowly degenerating

47
00:03:01,060 --> 00:03:04,140
into a state of total disorganization.

48
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:07,820
Thank God, I thought it was just me.

49
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:10,540
Yeah, so what are you guys doing tonight?

50
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:13,500
The House of Sweat?

51
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:20,180
Yeah, great. Hey, look, can I talk to you guys later?

52
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:24,600
Yeah, bye. Mike?

53
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:26,940
What is the House of Sweat?

54
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:30,020
Well, it's, you know...

55
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:33,200
It's that new under -20 dance club on Jericho Turnpike.

56
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:35,800
Yeah, and it sounds like a great idea, Mom.

57
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:39,840
Ah, it's a safe, wholesome place for teens to congregate.

58
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:43,740
And the larger the group, the smaller their brains get.

59
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:46,080
Oh, come on, Maggie.

60
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:48,180
Yeah, come on, Maggie.

61
00:03:50,340 --> 00:03:52,880
Ah, yes, well, time to go wait for that school bus.

62
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:55,760
You know, if I hurry, I can still get a seat in the non -smoking section.

63
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:58,420
Bye. Have a good day.

64
00:03:58,580 --> 00:04:00,200
Bye, sweetie. Have a good day.

65
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:02,380
Bye, Ben. I love you.

66
00:04:02,940 --> 00:04:06,760
Hello. Later, Ben.

67
00:04:09,140 --> 00:04:12,600
I still have some paperwork to do before my 9 o 'clock gets here.

68
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:18,960
And if you start feeling frisky and you have 8 or 10 seconds before work,

69
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:21,640
you know where to find me.

70
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:27,860
Ben, what are you doing here?

71
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:29,100
You'll miss the bus.

72
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:34,900
What's the matter, honey?

73
00:04:35,740 --> 00:04:37,460
Dad didn't know how to do my elbow.

74
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:40,960
Oh? Let me see.

75
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:46,660
Oh, Dad did a great job on this cut.

76
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:48,620
Superman Band -Aids, the works.

77
00:04:50,740 --> 00:04:52,280
Oh, I get it.

78
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:54,060
He didn't kiss it.

79
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:59,080
And say, I love you, little puppy.

80
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:02,120
It was all so clinical.

81
00:05:04,540 --> 00:05:09,200
Oh. Mom, how come you had to go back to work?

82
00:05:10,580 --> 00:05:12,300
I didn't have to, Ben.

83
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:14,120
I wanted to.

84
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:24,840
Come here. Ben, imagine you had to spend 15 years in this house without ever going

85
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:25,420
out to play.

86
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:27,260
You'd go crazy, wouldn't you?

87
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,460
Mm -hmm. Well, believe it or not, a lot of grown -ups feel the same way about work.

88
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:34,400
That's it, Mom.

89
00:05:34,780 --> 00:05:39,760
Oh, sure. And I know this has been a big change for all of us.

90
00:05:39,820 --> 00:05:44,300
And I worry about not being here for you because, well, you're the youngest.

91
00:05:45,260 --> 00:05:49,160
And I worry about not being here for Carol because she's a girl and she needs her

92
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:53,700
mother. And I worry about not being here for Mike to keep him from accidentally

93
00:05:53,700 --> 00:05:54,960
blowing something up.

94
00:05:56,200 --> 00:06:00,480
And believe me, I worry about leaving your father to cope with all you monsters.

95
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:03,640
You shouldn't worry so much, Mom.

96
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:05,540
You'll make yourself crazy.

97
00:06:07,500 --> 00:06:09,220
Die, you little punk.

98
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:16,560
And it's always the same dream, Doc.

99
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:25,460
I'm on a subway, and this woman sits across from me, this beautiful woman.

100
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:28,300
And I look at her,

101
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:30,590
she looks at me.

102
00:06:32,260 --> 00:06:33,740
I lick my lips.

103
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:38,240
She licks her lips.

104
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:40,680
This goes on.

105
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:45,060
And then finally she leans across and she whispers to me,

106
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:49,660
you have huge knees.

107
00:06:52,710 --> 00:06:54,240
Does that mean anything, Doc?

108
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:04,880
Oh. Well, that should be good for about five bucks a piece.

109
00:07:07,020 --> 00:07:08,000
Good visit, Walter.

110
00:07:08,580 --> 00:07:10,820
And don't worry too much about this thing, okay?

111
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:21,860
See you next week.

112
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,480
Bye -bye. Can I talk to you for a second, Dad?

113
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:29,180
Sure. In your office?

114
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:46,140
Sure. Kids. Me?

115
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:52,940
Yeah. So, you wanted to talk about something?

116
00:07:54,860 --> 00:07:59,340
Yeah, um, mostly I just wanted to mention how smoothly things have been running

117
00:07:59,340 --> 00:08:03,060
since the wife went back to work and you moved your practice into the house.

118
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:04,680
Well, thank you.

119
00:08:05,320 --> 00:08:08,660
Dad, we've been friends now for a long time, right?

120
00:08:09,420 --> 00:08:10,460
Off and on, yeah.

121
00:08:10,580 --> 00:08:11,440
Off and on.

122
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:12,440
I love that.

123
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:16,420
See, Dad, you know that dance hall place that I mentioned this morning?

124
00:08:16,760 --> 00:08:17,720
The house of sweat.

125
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:22,240
Yeah, yeah. Uh, Jerry and I were talking, and we decided to...

126
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:24,960
Jerry? Yeah, Jerry Delish.

127
00:08:25,660 --> 00:08:29,400
He's an older friend of mine, excellent driver, with two years of driver's ed.

128
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:31,400
Two years of driver's ed?

129
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:35,500
Yeah, see, in his first class, he ran over a dog.

130
00:08:36,080 --> 00:08:37,760
But he drove beautifully after that.

131
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:40,680
We're talking one tiny, reckless little dog here, Danny.

132
00:08:42,080 --> 00:08:43,920
Tough break. Yeah.

133
00:08:44,280 --> 00:08:47,880
So, anyway, I was thinking that maybe we could go down there tonight, and Jerry

134
00:08:47,880 --> 00:08:49,020
would drive, so you wouldn't have to do it.

135
00:08:49,020 --> 00:08:50,120
What would your mother say?

136
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:56,120
Mom? I guess you would say...

137
00:08:58,240 --> 00:08:59,660
What's the phrase I'm looking for here, Dad?

138
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:01,600
No. Yeah, that's right.

139
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:04,060
I guess that means I can't go, right?

140
00:09:05,320 --> 00:09:08,580
Well, it just means I don't like you coming in here and trying to get away with

141
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:11,760
something. That's not the relationship I want to have with you.

142
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:13,920
I'm sorry, Dad.

143
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:15,760
All right, now look.

144
00:09:16,220 --> 00:09:18,820
Now that I'm in charge at home, we can try things my way.

145
00:09:18,820 --> 00:09:23,480
All right. You don't even know what my way is.

146
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:24,700
Sure I do, Dad.

147
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:25,760
It's a Sinatra song.

148
00:09:28,120 --> 00:09:29,860
You're walking a fine line here, Mike.

149
00:09:31,260 --> 00:09:32,480
Okay, look, here's the deal.

150
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:35,620
I'll give you a little more freedom.

151
00:09:36,480 --> 00:09:38,800
You've got to promise me a lot more responsibility.

152
00:09:39,380 --> 00:09:40,360
Hey, no problem, Dad.

153
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:42,700
I swear. I am ready for total responsibility.

154
00:09:43,420 --> 00:09:45,440
Mike, I'm not ready for total responsibility.

155
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:47,500
You're right. Sorry.

156
00:09:48,040 --> 00:09:50,500
Okay. You go out and have a good time.

157
00:09:51,380 --> 00:09:52,780
Just remember what we talked about.

158
00:09:52,980 --> 00:09:54,640
Absolutely, Dad. Thanks, I promise.

159
00:09:58,360 --> 00:09:59,420
Wait, what about Mom?

160
00:09:59,440 --> 00:10:00,320
What if she's mad?

161
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:02,360
Mike, your mom's not an ogre.

162
00:10:03,220 --> 00:10:04,080
I'll talk to her.

163
00:10:04,080 --> 00:10:06,800
She'll understand. You let him do what?

164
00:10:08,460 --> 00:10:10,540
Maggie, he's 15 years old now.

165
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:12,380
So he's 15.

166
00:10:12,580 --> 00:10:15,880
It's completely arbitrary to just pick an age like that and say that is when a kid

167
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:20,200
is mature. You know, by the time Mozart was 15, he'd written seven symphonies?

168
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:24,040
That's because Mozart's father didn't let him go to the House of Sweat.

169
00:10:27,080 --> 00:10:28,540
Who did he go with?

170
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:29,960
I don't know, some kid.

171
00:10:30,040 --> 00:10:33,580
Jerry Dillish. Jerry Dog Killer.

172
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:39,500
Dillish. Maggie, he hit one dog.

173
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:43,560
Yeah, but he hit it four times.

174
00:10:46,520 --> 00:10:52,700
Okay, well, uh, Mike isn't Jerry, and a kid needs some freedom in order to learn

175
00:10:52,700 --> 00:10:59,080
responsibility. Oh, Jason, I know you believe in this unlimited human potential

176
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:03,440
stuff. And that's great for your patience, but when it comes...

177
00:11:03,440 --> 00:11:04,480
Comes to our own children.

178
00:11:04,520 --> 00:11:06,100
I believe in original sin.

179
00:11:06,420 --> 00:11:12,400
Sin. I don't know, maybe I shouldn't have gone back to work.

180
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:14,960
Now, come on, Meggie, don't say that.

181
00:11:15,340 --> 00:11:19,640
Now, you took 15 years off to raise a family, and you deserve to go back to work

182
00:11:19,640 --> 00:11:23,120
now. You just have to have a little more faith than me and the kids.

183
00:11:25,680 --> 00:11:27,240
Oh, maybe you're right.

184
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:28,880
Of course I'm right.

185
00:11:29,820 --> 00:11:30,760
We shouldn't be worrying.

186
00:11:30,860 --> 00:11:33,340
We should be celebrating.

187
00:11:34,440 --> 00:11:39,300
Which is why I've taken the liberty of placing some chilled champagne in a little

188
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:40,540
bucket beside the bed,

189
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:43,880
Slipped some satin sheets on the old bouncy?

190
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:47,960
Satin sheets. You?

191
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:53,840
Yeah. Yeah, well, the guy in the store showed me some before and after pictures of a

192
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:55,280
couple who tried them, and...

193
00:11:57,040 --> 00:11:58,380
they looked very satisfied.

194
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:02,500
What about Ben and Carol?

195
00:12:02,940 --> 00:12:05,780
Well, I slipped some sleeping pills into their Gatorade.

196
00:12:07,120 --> 00:12:08,600
They'll be asleep for about three weeks.

197
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:12,980
Jason. Well, I didn't really, but they are.

198
00:12:13,620 --> 00:12:14,480
And we can...

199
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:16,740
Right then. Mm.

200
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:23,400
Mm. Hello? Yeah, this is Jason Siever.

201
00:12:24,780 --> 00:12:27,260
No, you must be looking for someone else, because...

202
00:12:27,260 --> 00:12:28,260
Take your clothes off.

203
00:12:29,860 --> 00:12:33,420
No, no, our mic is only 15, so he wouldn't be driving a car.

204
00:12:36,860 --> 00:12:42,220
I see. What did he say?

205
00:12:42,780 --> 00:12:45,500
He said, that's why your mic is in our jail.

206
00:13:02,900 --> 00:13:04,660
What are you in for, kid?

207
00:13:04,660 --> 00:13:08,880
I, um... I killed a man just to watch him die.

208
00:13:12,940 --> 00:13:15,960
You? I'm paid parking tickets.

209
00:13:17,160 --> 00:13:18,420
No, it's my mom.

210
00:13:18,980 --> 00:13:20,060
Come on, Maggie.

211
00:13:20,140 --> 00:13:21,780
We don't even know the facts yet.

212
00:13:22,160 --> 00:13:26,240
I mean, it's not so unusual for a teenage boy to have a minor run -in with the

213
00:13:26,240 --> 00:13:30,140
police. Some of these guys can be real macho headbangers.

214
00:13:31,340 --> 00:13:33,900
Hiya. Folks care for some hot cocoa?

215
00:13:33,900 --> 00:13:34,900
Oh, I just made a fresh pot.

216
00:13:36,280 --> 00:13:37,320
Look, we're the Sievers.

217
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:38,800
You've locked up our son.

218
00:13:39,380 --> 00:13:41,440
An officer claimed he was driving a car.

219
00:13:41,820 --> 00:13:42,840
Uh, yes, sir.

220
00:13:43,240 --> 00:13:46,100
We picked him up in the House of Sweat parking lot.

221
00:13:46,680 --> 00:13:49,780
He'd been driving in circles for approximately 12 minutes.

222
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:55,120
Okay, so a 15 -year -old boy drives his friend's car around the lot a few times.

223
00:13:55,740 --> 00:13:58,720
Oh, did I mention he sideswiped a police car on the way out?

224
00:13:59,380 --> 00:14:02,880
He what? He tore that bumper off like he was peeling an orange.

225
00:14:03,900 --> 00:14:06,660
A $350 orange.

226
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:15,380
Hiya, Dad, Mom.

227
00:14:16,340 --> 00:14:17,680
You look good tonight.

228
00:14:19,280 --> 00:14:21,220
You look young.

229
00:14:22,900 --> 00:14:24,020
Come on, son.

230
00:14:25,340 --> 00:14:26,600
Mom, Dad, this is Jerry.

231
00:14:30,680 --> 00:14:33,840
I guess it's kind of hard to see the basis of our friendship, huh?

232
00:14:36,900 --> 00:14:37,760
I don't know.

233
00:14:37,780 --> 00:14:40,360
He has a certain carefree charm.

234
00:14:41,620 --> 00:14:42,900
I should see him when he's sober.

235
00:14:43,440 --> 00:14:46,840
Mike, you will be grounded for two months.

236
00:14:47,620 --> 00:14:50,720
Two months? Dad, can't you talk to her?

237
00:14:51,220 --> 00:14:52,260
Oh, I did, Mike.

238
00:14:52,880 --> 00:14:54,140
Originally, it was one month.

239
00:14:54,920 --> 00:14:56,260
What do you mean, you added a month?

240
00:14:56,660 --> 00:14:58,720
Nothing gets by you, does it?

241
00:15:00,780 --> 00:15:01,900
Dad, he said you talked...

242
00:15:01,900 --> 00:15:03,760
Damn it, Mike, you said you could act responsibly.

243
00:15:04,080 --> 00:15:05,480
Now, I don't want to hear another word out of you.

244
00:15:05,480 --> 00:15:06,080
Is that clear?

245
00:15:42,340 --> 00:15:46,260
Oh, yeah. Our romantic evening.

246
00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:52,360
Anyone who's not used to satin sheets could easily have an accident.

247
00:15:52,360 --> 00:15:54,080
Maggie, don't patronize me, okay?

248
00:15:57,440 --> 00:15:59,060
And where the hell are my pajamas?

249
00:15:59,720 --> 00:16:01,700
Uh, gee, I'm sorry.

250
00:16:01,820 --> 00:16:03,300
I'm really not sure.

251
00:16:03,540 --> 00:16:07,780
Well, you wouldn't think it would be so damn tough to keep tabs on a pair of pajamas

252
00:16:07,780 --> 00:16:10,760
around here. Jason, I don't understand why you're so upset.

253
00:16:10,860 --> 00:16:13,880
I mean, it's not like this is the first time he's screwed up.

254
00:16:14,140 --> 00:16:14,960
Who screwed up?

